{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792423292,"nonce":"kJlWJ73v1qvj3Vkra+dAz8SNFwY=","title":"Bound scrolling graph canvases to their viewport","message":"Opening a long expanded history crashes RayDE with a GPU texture validation error: the graph's full data height becomes the canvas texture height (reported 14623 against an 8192-pixel device limit). Native scrolling must use a nonpainting extent spacer, while CanvasKit paints a viewport-sized window with content-coordinate hit testing. Cull offscreen paths, shapes, text and virtual hit regions. Clamp offsets when filtering reduces the scene.\n\nRegression: a 1000-row, 24000-unit retained scene must render in a viewport-sized canvas, scroll to its final row, dispatch the final row's content coordinates, index only visible rows, and clamp to the top when reduced to one row. Run dark/light Git snapshots and inspect the preview. Keep generic pan/zoom canvas behavior for other extension consumers.","files":null}]}